                <h2>Step 1: Identify the Suspension Reason First</h2>
<p>Before preparing any appeal materials, log in to your Google Ads account and locate the suspension notification. Google typically sends a policy violation email titled "Your Google Ads account has been suspended." Key steps:</p>
<ul>
<li>Review the email notification carefully — it specifies the violation type and links to relevant policies.</li>
<li>Check <strong>Account Settings → Account Preferences</strong> for status codes.</li>
<li>Common suspension reasons: intellectual property complaints, misleading ads, policy violations (prohibited content), payment issues (excessive chargebacks), or suspicious traffic patterns.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Note</strong>: If the suspension is due to "repeated policy violations after multiple warnings," the appeal becomes significantly harder. It is strongly recommended to fully correct your ads and landing pages before submitting an appeal.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Gather and Organize Evidence</h2>
<p>The success of your appeal largely depends on the quality and clarity of your evidence. Prepare the following checklist:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Business registration documents</strong>: Proof of business identity (PDF format, clearly readable).</li>
<li><strong>Domain ownership proof</strong>: WHOIS records or DNS configuration screenshots from your hosting provider.</li>
<li><strong>Compliance documentation</strong>: Industry-specific certifications, licenses, or authorizations for regulated sectors (finance, healthcare, gaming).</li>
<li><strong>Original ad creatives and translations</strong>: Provide copies of the flagged ad copy, landing page screenshots, and English translations.</li>
<li><strong>Remediation records</strong>: Screenshots showing before-and-after comparisons of modified or deleted ads, with timestamps.</li>
<li><strong>Payment proof</strong>: Recent advertising invoices or billing statements (last three months) demonstrating normal account activity.</li>
</ol>
<p>Organize all files in a single folder, naming them using the format: <code>Date_ViolationType_MaterialName</code>.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Draft Your Appeal</h2>
<p>Google Ads official appeals are submitted via the <a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/troubleshooter/4578507">Policy Support Page</a>. Select "Account suspended" → "Submit an appeal."</p>
<p><strong>Recommended email structure:</strong></p>
<pre><code>Subject: Appeal Request for Account [Your Account ID] – [Violation Type]

1. Brief greeting + Account ID
2. Acknowledge the violation (quote the official notification)
3. List remediation steps taken
4. Reference attached materials (by name)
5. Commitment to future compliance
6. Request for account reactivation
</code></pre>
<p><strong>Practical tips:</strong><br />
- Keep the tone professional and objective. Avoid confrontational language such as "Why did you ban my account?"<br />
- Do not submit duplicate appeals for the same issue — wait <strong>5-7 business days</strong> before resubmitting.<br />
- If your first appeal is rejected, carefully review the rejection reason, supplement new evidence, and submit an updated appeal.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Track Your Appeal</h2>
<p>Google typically responds within <strong>3-5 business days</strong>. Track progress via:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Email response</strong>: The policy support team will reply to the email address used during submission.</li>
<li><strong>Google Ads interface</strong>: Some results are also reflected in account status.</li>
<li><strong>Follow up</strong>: If no response after 10 business days, add a follow-up comment in the original form or contact your dedicated Google account manager.</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Step 5: Prevention Best Practices</h2>
<p>Appeals are a reactive measure. Prevention is always better:</p>
<ul>
<li>Review the <strong>Policy Details</strong> page monthly and address warnings promptly.</li>
<li>Use the <a href="https://ads.google.com/aw/policychecker">Google Ads Policy Checker</a> before launching new creatives.</li>
<li>Maintain stable payment information to avoid sudden chargebacks.</li>
<li>Ensure landing page content matches your ad promises — avoid exaggerated or misleading claims.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<blockquote>
<p><strong>YiYu Media Tip</strong>: If you manage multiple Google Ads accounts simultaneously, consider using separate domains and payment methods to isolate accounts. This prevents one suspension from affecting your other active campaigns.</p>
</blockquote>
